---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/intermediate/organic-chemistry-hydrocarbons">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Organic chemistry is carbon's story. Carbon bonds to itself endlessly — chains, branches,
    rings — creating more possible compounds than every other element combined (tens of
    millions). The simplest families are the <strong>hydrocarbons</strong>: carbon and hydrogen
    only. Everything from natural gas to candle wax to the aromatics in paint thinner is one of
    them, differing by length and by how the carbons connect.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="one structural change, one property shift — longer chains mean heavier, higher-boiling liquids" widget="plotter" config={boilCfg} />

  <p>
    Methane (CH₄) is a gas; octane (C₈H₁₈) a volatile liquid; C₂₀+ chains are waxy solids. Same
    bonds, one trend: longer chains have more surface area to stick together with, so more
    energy (heat) is needed to boil them. This single trend is why an oil refinery's distillation
    tower works — crude oil sorts itself by chain length as it rises through cooling trays.
  </p>

  <h2>Three families to know</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Alkanes</strong> — all single bonds, saturated, famously unreactive (except burning). CₙH₂ₙ₊₂: methane, propane, octane, candle wax.</li>
    <li><strong>Alkenes</strong> — one double bond, CₙH₂ₙ: reactive, and the feedstock of plastics. Ethene → polyethylene, the world's most-made polymer.</li>
    <li><strong>Aromatics</strong> — benzene rings whose six electrons delocalise into a uniquely stable ring. The stability ("aromaticity") shapes drug design, dyes and the octane rating of petrol.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Isomers: same formula, different molecule</h2>
  <p>
    C₄H₁₀ can be butane or isobutane — different shapes, different boiling points. Longer chains
    multiply the possibilities exponentially, which is why organic chemistry is a subject and not
    a chapter: structure, not just composition, decides behaviour.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Organic Chemistry; Clayden, Greeves & Warren, Organic Chemistry.</p>
</TopicPage>
